//? Title: Clarke–Wright Savings: Building Capacity-Feasible Delivery Routes
//? Subtitle: Actual endpoint decisions, distance accounting, deterministic ties and a greedy counterexample
//? Exports: both
//? Lang: en-GB

\abstract(
Given a depot, customer locations, demands and a common vehicle capacity, find
closed delivery routes that serve each customer once without overloading a
vehicle. The number of possible partitions and visit orders makes exact search
expensive. Clarke–Wright savings starts with separate depot trips and greedily
joins compatible route endpoints. We retain the original three-point example,
inspect actual native decisions and distance savings, and exhibit a small
Euclidean problem where a different feasible routing is shorter. Checked C++
validation, independent route evaluation and complete work caps make the
demonstrated contracts explicit without turning the heuristic into an optimizer.
)

\toc

> The original example: replacing two depot trips

Let the depot be vertex 0 at (0,0), with customers 1 and 2 at (1,0) and (2,0).
Each customer demands one unit, and capacity is two. Separate routes
0→1→0 and 0→2→0 have total distance 2+4=6. Joining the customers replaces
edges 1→0 and 0→2 by edge 1→2, reducing distance by 1+2−1=2.
The returned route 0→1→2→0 has load two and distance four.

The native diagram is an itinerary, not a geographic map: positions separate
visits so both initial routes and the accepted concatenation remain readable.
The merged customer order and cost accounting come from the executing kernel.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/clarke_wright_method.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  const auto trace=dm::algorithms::clarke_wright_trace({{0,0},{1,0},{2,0}},{0,1,1},2);
  const auto& step=trace.steps[0];
  std::print("| original route result | value |\n| :--- | :--- |\n| closed route | {} |\n| route load | {} |\n| initial separate-trip length | {} |\n| accepted saving | {} |\n| measured final length | {} |\n",trace.result.routes[0],trace.route_loads[0],trace.initial_star_distance,trace.accepted_savings,trace.result.total_distance);
  dm::graphics::Diagram figure(60,1100);
  dm::graphics::DiagramNodeStyle node;node.width=1.5;node.height=1.0;node.font_size=21;
  for(int row=0;row<2;++row) {
    const int customer=row+1;const double y=3.0-2.5*row;
    for(int visit=0;visit<3;++visit) {
      const auto id=std::format("before{}_{}",row,visit);
      figure.node(id,{3.0*visit,y},visit==1?std::to_string(customer):"D",node);
      if(visit)figure.edge(std::format("before{}_{}",row,visit-1),id);
    }
    figure.text({9,y},std::format("separate trip: load 1, length {}",2*customer),20);
  }
  node.fill={220,252,231};const auto& route=trace.result.routes[0];
  for(std::size_t visit=0;visit<route.size();++visit) {
    const auto id=std::format("after{}",visit);
    figure.node(id,{3.0*visit,-3.5},route[visit]?std::to_string(route[visit]):"D",node);
    if(visit)figure.edge(std::format("after{}",visit-1),id);
  }
  figure.text({5.5,5},"Replace two depot edges by one customer edge",23)
        .text({5.5,-1.4},std::format("join ({},{}): saving {}, combined load {}",step.i,step.j,step.saving,step.load_i+step.load_j),21)
        .text({5.5,-5.3},std::format("one returned route; measured distance {}",trace.result.total_distance),21)
        .save_svg("build/clarke_wright_method.svg");
  if(trace.result.routes.size()!=1 || trace.result.total_distance!=4)return 1;
}
```
