> The saving formula and the parallel variant

For symmetric Euclidean distances d, consider distinct routes ending at i and
starting at j. Reversing a route changes no undirected edge lengths. Remove
their depot edges and insert edge ij. The reduction in total distance is

$$
s_{ij}=d_{0i}+d_{0j}-d_{ij}.
$$
\alttext(The saving from joining endpoints i and j is the sum of their two removed depot distances minus the new customer-to-customer distance.)

Triangle inequality makes this saving nonnegative in exact Euclidean arithmetic.
The implementation caches depot distances, constructs every unordered customer
pair, and sorts descending by its widened saving, breaking equal savings by
ascending original pair ids (i,j). It processes zero savings too, and does not
filter tiny rounded negative savings. Rounded arithmetic is not an exact
triangle-inequality or monotone-distance certificate.

This is the parallel savings variant: the whole sorted pair list is considered
once while many routes may grow. A pair can merge only if both customers are
currently endpoints of distinct routes and their combined load fits capacity.
After orientation, route_i ends at i and route_j starts at j; concatenation
retains slot route_i and clears slot route_j. Stable slots are assigned in
ascending customer-id order, excluding the chosen depot. Final routes appear
in ascending surviving-slot order and receive depot boundaries at the end.

The model uses a common finite positive capacity and finite nonnegative demands.
Each customer demand must fit individually. The depot demand is validated but
ignored, and a depot-only problem needs no route. Zero-demand customers are
still customers and must be visited. There is no fixed fleet size, delivery
splitting, time window, road network, improvement phase or optimality guarantee.

> Why the returned routes are feasible

Initially every customer forms its own route, so uniqueness and individual
capacity hold. An accepted merge joins two disjoint customer sequences at
endpoints, preserving every visit exactly once; rejecting interior endpoints
prevents branching within a route. Requiring distinct routes prevents a
premature customer-only cycle. The checked load sum preserves capacity.
Adding depot visits closes each surviving route. This induction explains
feasibility; it does not show that the chosen partition or visit order is best.

If S is the sum of accepted savings, the exact-arithmetic accounting identity is

$$
L_{final}=2\sum_i d_{0i}-S.
$$
\alttext(Final total route length equals the initial depot-star distance minus the savings of accepted merges, in exact arithmetic.)

The trace records both this accounting and a fresh final edge sum, with their
widened residual. The ordinary and traced calls share one executing kernel;
recording does not replay merges. Widened geometry and loads are checked before
finite-double final cost storage. Last-bit costs and earlier unspecified tie
choices can therefore differ from the old unchecked implementation.

> Endpoint decisions on four collinear customers

Put four unit-demand customers at x=1,2,3,4 with depot x=0 and capacity four.
The best initial saving is six for pair (3,4), followed by the tied savings
four for (2,3) and (2,4). The first two joins build sequence [2,3,4]; pair
(2,4) is rejected because its endpoints already share that route. After joining
customer 1, pair (1,3) is rejected because customer 3 is interior.

The table contains every actual decision. The diagram retains accepted orders
only, and its arrows mean the next accepted merge; rejected pairs are not hidden
from the table. Original-id tie ordering makes this walkthrough reproducible.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/clarke_wright_endpoints.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  const auto trace=dm::algorithms::clarke_wright_trace({{0,0},{1,0},{2,0},{3,0},{4,0}},{0,1,1,1,1},4);
  std::print("| pair | saving | endpoint slots | reason | committed customer order |\n| :--- | ---: | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n");
  dm::graphics::Diagram figure(55,1100);
  dm::graphics::DiagramNodeStyle style;style.width=5.8;style.height=2.8;style.font_size=19;style.fill={220,252,231};
  std::size_t accepted=0;
  for(const auto& step:trace.steps) {
    const auto reason=step.decision==dm::algorithms::ClarkeWrightDecision::Merged?"merged":step.decision==dm::algorithms::ClarkeWrightDecision::MissingEndpoint?"interior endpoint":step.decision==dm::algorithms::ClarkeWrightDecision::SameRoute?"same route":"capacity";
    std::print("| ({},{}) | {:.1f} | {}, {} | {} | {} |\n",step.i,step.j,step.saving,step.route_i?std::to_string(*step.route_i):"absent",step.route_j?std::to_string(*step.route_j):"absent",reason,step.merged_customers);
    if(step.decision!=dm::algorithms::ClarkeWrightDecision::Merged)continue;
    const auto id=std::format("merge{}",accepted);
    figure.node(id,{8.0*accepted,0},std::format("join ({},{})\ncustomers {}\nload {:.0f}",step.i,step.j,step.merged_customers,step.load_i+step.load_j),style);
    if(accepted)figure.edge(std::format("merge{}",accepted-1),id,"next accepted");
    ++accepted;
  }
  figure.text({8,3},"Actual endpoint-compatible route growth",23)
        .text({8,-3},std::format("{} merges; {} candidate decisions; final length {}",trace.work.merges,trace.work.decisions,trace.result.total_distance),20)
        .save_svg("build/clarke_wright_endpoints.svg");
  std::print("\nInitial length {}; accepted savings {}; measured length {}; accounting residual {:.2e}.\n",trace.initial_star_distance,trace.accepted_savings,trace.measured_distance,trace.accounting_residual);
}
